    <entry>
        <title type="html"><![CDATA[Clean up `AccentColor` and `AccentColorText` data]]></title>
        <id>https://github.com/mdn/browser-compat-data/pull/30637</id>
        <link href="https://github.com/mdn/browser-compat-data/pull/30637"/>
        <updated>2026-09-23T17:28:30.000Z</updated>
        <content type="html"><![CDATA[<img src="https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/64103?u=428d3c27c9cd7efc648e17dfe5a4321c3f8e44d2&v=4" width="64" height="64" alt=""/><br/>
<h4 dir="auto">Summary</h4>
<p dir="auto">Make the <code class="notranslate">AccentColor</code> and <code class="notranslate">AccentColorText</code> data more regular.</p>

<h4 dir="auto">Test results and supporting details</h4>
<ul dir="auto">
<li>Split up <code class="notranslate">AccentColor</code> and <code class="notranslate">AccentColorText</code>. This makes working with the notes much less confusing, since we have to cover just one behavior at a time.</li>
<li>Chrome desktop:
<ul dir="auto">
<li>Shipped the values in version 149, not 150. Confirmed with <a href="https://codepen.io/leaverou/pen/YPpNwvm" rel="nofollow">a test case from Lea Verou</a> — at least for the white/blue anti-fingeprinting behavior.</li>
<li>Revised the notes to focus on what the actual behavior is (using a fixed value). <em>Why</em> they did it is maybe something we could mention, but I don't think we typically do this for <a href="https://drafts.csswg.org/css-color/#css-system-colors:~:text=User%20agents%20may,user" rel="nofollow">well-specified behavior</a>.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>Chrome Android:
<ul dir="auto">
<li>I don't think either value shipped on Android. Lea's test case didn't work for me and it's corroborated by <a href="https://chromestatus.com/feature/5068127364186112" rel="nofollow">https://chromestatus.com/feature/5068127364186112</a>.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>Firefox: This seems to just work!</li>
<li>Safari: The original note is a mess (unclear plus spelling and punctuation issues). My own testing seemed to show that Safari just uses the black-on-blue values (changing the setting in the note text had no obvious behavior). Safari on iOS in BrowserStack didn't show anything special (black on blue).</li>
</ul>


<h4 dir="auto">Related issues</h4>
<ul dir="auto">
<li>Initiated by <a class="issue-link js-issue-link" data-error-text="Failed to load title" data-id="5301684684" data-permission-text="Title is private" data-url="https://github.com/web-platform-dx/web-features/issues/4284" data-hovercard-type="issue" data-hovercard-url="/web-platform-dx/web-features/issues/4284/hovercard" href="https://github.com/web-platform-dx/web-features/issues/4284">web-platform-dx/web-features#4284</a></li>
<li>Previously: <a class="issue-link js-issue-link" data-error-text="Failed to load title" data-id="1974384365" data-permission-text="Title is private" data-url="https://github.com/mdn/browser-compat-data/issues/21150" data-hovercard-type="pull_request" data-hovercard-url="/mdn/browser-compat-data/pull/21150/hovercard" href="https://github.com/mdn/browser-compat-data/pull/21150">#21150</a></li>
<li>Previously: <a class="issue-link js-issue-link" data-error-text="Failed to load title" data-id="4857269991" data-permission-text="Title is private" data-url="https://github.com/mdn/browser-compat-data/issues/30025" data-hovercard-type="pull_request" data-hovercard-url="/mdn/browser-compat-data/pull/30025/hovercard" href="https://github.com/mdn/browser-compat-data/pull/30025">#30025</a></li>
</ul>

